+## 2026.11.11a
+
+**Release B2, machine-readable headers on the dual-read records** (the bridge's second change cycle, begun 2026-11-04, its design and plan addendum approved at one gate that day as the subject `release-b2`; built on the bridge's own branches in stages H1 and H2, stage H2's review of this text before the stamp): each of the eight records programs read, the status file, every build plan, every brief, the inventory, the register, the diagram index, the deviations file and the run parameters, gains a YAML front-matter header the programs read first; the prose stays, for the owner and for every instruction that quotes it, and the record's own checker fails a header that disagrees with it; a new program, `records-migrate.py`, writes each record's header from its prose. The programs that change: `bridge_env.py` (the reader, the schemas and the one copy of every fallback reading), `loop-guard.py`, `session-start.py`, `conformance-check.py`, `workflow-check.py`, `inventory-check.py`, `evaluate-reports.py`, `plan-check.py`, `scope-check.py`, `completion-check.py`, `diagram-check.py`, `bridge-run.py` and `review-guard.py`. Against 2026.11.09a: one file new (`records-migrate.py`), none removed, one allow entry new.
+
+**What changed**
+- **The header reader and the schemas** (`bridge_env.py`; R-057, TASK-046, TASK-050): `read_header` reads a stated subset of YAML and refuses, naming the line, every form outside it, a plain value YAML would type (a date, `yes`, a number with a point) among them, to be double-quoted; `HEADER_SCHEMAS` holds the eight kinds' keys and types (D-005); `check_header` lists a header's schema faults and `header_agrees` the keys whose values differ from the prose's reading; every field two programs read two ways is read by one copy in `bridge_env.py`, the eleven second readings retired and a test holding that no program keeps one; `pyyaml` stays a test-only dependency.
+- **The status file's header, read first** (R-058, TASK-047): by the loop guard, the session-start hook, the conformance, workflow and inventory checks and the evaluation, each falling back to the prose for a file with no header; the inventory check's caps count the body after the header.
+- **The plan's and the briefs' headers** (R-059, TASK-048): `plan-check.py` compares each increment's and stage's header fields with the prose, exempts a `historic: true` increment the merged set holds from the shape rules and reports an unbalanced parenthesis in an unmerged increment's Scope; `scope-check.py` compares every brief key with its prose or the file's name; `completion-check.py` reads `satisfies` from the plan's header; the launcher and the guard refuse a malformed or disagreeing brief header.
+- **The tabular records' headers** (R-060, TASK-049): the inventory, the register, the index and the deviations file declare their sections, columns and role columns, never their rows; each table's owning checker compares the declaration with the table, and every reader splits a row by the declared columns.
+- **The run parameters' header** (R-061, TASK-050): the five values and the four owner lines programs read, read first by the launcher, the review guard and the loop guard, each lift compared with its line before it governs a launch, a snapshot or a stop.
+- **The migration, a program and a text** (R-062; TASK-051, TASK-052, BUG-044): `records-migrate.py [--check] [--base <ref>]` writes each header-less record's header from its prose, each write atomic and read back through the reader, the schema and the agreement before it replaces the record; `--check` writes nothing and lists what it would write, what it cannot and what has a header; a plan its headed readers would refuse, an increment heading outside any stage or an R-id on a Satisfies line the header would not hold, is listed `cannot read` and left as it is (BUG-044); the templates of the eight records carry the header, the core's "Project state" states the rule, and calibration's step 4 runs the migration.
+- **This repository's records** (R-063, TASK-054, the increment after this stamp): migrated by the installed program at this project's calibration to the release.
+- **The release** (R-064, TASK-053): this stamp, its test and its run sheet.
+
+**Running projects must**
+- *(all phases)* Install with every project paused at a merge boundary, everything committed, restart, run `/calibrate-bridge` before the next increment, as 2026.10.30b's entry says. On the real install the installer ends 0 with `RESULT: INSTALLED TREE MATCHES bridge 2026.11.11a`; over 2026.11.09a installed whole it also prints its `previous` line, `previous 2026.11.09a kept at <folder>`. The installer keeps one release as the way back.
+- *(all phases)* The text is in force from the calibration, reopening no gate: re-read as calibration's step 2 says; the `Calibrated:` line is rewritten, the earlier text living in git.
+- *(all phases)* The migration is a record-keeping addition of calibration's step 4, made at the next checkpoint: `python ~/.claude/cursor-bridge/records-migrate.py --check` from the Workspace root first, its lines in the calibration's report; a status file listed `cannot write` is trimmed first, since an older release's check counts header and body together against 12,000 characters and 120 lines; each repairable `cannot read` record is repaired in its prose and the check run again; a merged brief the program cannot read is never repaired: it stays header-less and is named in the report; then the migration itself, after which `--check` lists nothing to write, the result committed as the calibration's checkpoint in a records-only commit of its own. Every record written from then on is written from its template, header and all.
+- *(all phases)* From the migration on, a record's header and its prose say the same thing: a prose line a header mirrors is edited with its header key in the same commit. The record's checker prints `HEADER DISAGREES: <key>` where they differ, a finding (exit 1) fixed before the next checkpoint, and `HEADER MALFORMED: <path>:<line> <what>` for a header the reader refuses or the schema fails, exit 2, the check unable to verify until the header is repaired: at the line the message names for a header that cannot be read, at the keys it names for one that breaks its schema; the status file's caps count the body after the header.
+- *(building, changing)* The launcher's and the guard's `refused - header: ` (a brief's or the run parameters' header malformed or disagreeing with its prose) is a stop, never a model's failure: the header is corrected to agree with the prose and the launch or the snapshot made again. An owner's lift line is mirrored in the run parameters' header, and a lift in the header that no owner's line holds lifts nothing. The session-start hook's `header malformed: <path>:<line>` and `header disagrees: <key>`, and the loop guard's refused stop on `header disagrees: <key>`, are fixed in the record before the next action.
+- *(planning, building, changing)* A plan increment marked `historic: true` is a merged increment written before the plan's fields existed: `plan-check.py` exempts it from the shape rules while the merged set holds its ID, and a mark on an unmerged increment is a finding.
+- *(all phases)* The retired second readings: where two programs read one field two ways, the reading kept is the stricter one (the `Workflows:` line in the workflow check's grammar, the register's R-ids from its id column alone, a brief's Scope as `scope-check.py` reads it), so a record only a retired reading accepted shows as a finding at the first check after the calibration, fixed in its prose, reopening no gate.
+- *(all phases)* The `Bridge version:` line is written by calibration's step 5 alone. A project whose line already reads 2026.11.11a, this bridge's own among them, takes the no-delta path, which runs no step 4; this repository's records are migrated by its TASK-054.
+
+**If rolled back**
+- *(all phases)* `bridge-install.py --rollback` restores the kept copy of 2026.11.09a: it removes `records-migrate.py`, the one file only this release ships, and its allow entry, `Bash(python ~/.claude/cursor-bridge/records-migrate.py:*)`, so the program falls to the classifier until the reinstall. The older programs know nothing of the header and read the prose, but four of them read lines of the header as prose lines, since their readers ignore case and the header comes first: `loop-guard.py` and `session-start.py` read the status header's `phase:` line as its `Phase:` line, `conformance-check.py` reads that line and the header's `stage:` line as `Phase:` and `Stage:`, and `completion-check.py`'s search reads the plan header's `satisfies:` lists beside the prose's `Satisfies:` lines. Observed on 2026-11-11 over a scratch copy of this repository's records in the system temporary folder, never the live records, shaped first as TASK-054 will shape this repository's (the status file's `Working files:` line made one sentence, 15 of the plan's `Scope:` lines repaired) and migrated by this release's program (121 records written; the three merged briefs it cannot read left header-less), where header and prose held the same values, the installed 2026.11.09a checkers printed the same lines before and after the migration for `completion-check.py --mode plan` and `--mode done`, `plan-check.py`, `diagram-check.py --mode design` and `diagram-check.py --mode code`, `workflow-check.py --status`, `session-start.py` and `loop-guard.py`: the header's `satisfies:` lists carry the same IDs as the prose's `Satisfies:` lines, so `completion-check.py`'s search finds the same set. `inventory-check.py` counts the status file whole, `note: status: 39 lines / 9943 chars (cap 120 / 12000)` before the migration and `note: status: 52 lines / 11986 chars (cap 120 / 12000)` after it, under both caps, since the migration refuses a status file that would exceed either. `conformance-check.py` reads the header's `stage:` line as the status file's `Stage:`, its opening quote with it, and adds one NOTE, its line ending `is not the status file's ("release b2's stage h1 closed 2026-11-11): bring it up to date at this reflection point`, the count moving from `conformance-check: 24 OK, 0 MISSING, 2 NOTE` to `conformance-check: 24 OK, 0 MISSING, 3 NOTE`; while rolled back, that NOTE is the header's quote and not a stale `project.json`.
+- *(all phases)* While rolled back, a change of the status file's phase or stage is written in the header's `phase:` or `stage:` line as well as its prose line, and a plan increment's `Satisfies:` line with its header's `satisfies:` list: the older loop guard and session-start hook act on the header's phase, so a phase changed in the prose alone (`done` at project end, say) leaves the loop guard refusing stops as in the old phase and the hook naming the old phase's modules. Every other header line stays in its record, read as a field by no older program; a prose line edited while rolled back without its header key gives `HEADER DISAGREES: <key>` at the reinstall, fixed by bringing the header to the prose. A brief packaged under the older template carries no header and is read as before.
+
+**Field acceptance**
+- The TDP, the trial, pauses once at a merge boundary, calibrates and resumes: its calibration's `records-migrate.py --check` lines in its report, every record it can read migrated in one records-only commit, `--check` listing nothing to write after it, its checks green with no `HEADER` line, the increments written before the plan's fields existed marked `historic: true` and its plan check's shape noise about them gone, no gate reopened. While it has no increment at the gate, that migration and its checks stand in; its first brief packaged and launched under the new template is recorded when its next increment comes. reAngle and the video factory calibrate when the owner resumes them (R-031, R-037, deferred by the owner on 2026-10-31).
+
+**For the maintainer**
+- Program items, none in this release: `pyyaml` at run time once the installed programs have a declared environment; a header for `run/supervisor/in-flight.md` and the SCOPE files where a second reader appears; the leads recorded in this repository's `docs/HARDENING.md` under TASK-046 to TASK-052, BUG-044 and stage H2's review, round 2 (among them two of calibration's step 4: its exit-3 stop names no repair for a failed temporary write, read or replace, and its list of `cannot read` repairs reads as complete while the program prints other reasons too).
+- Two halves outside `bridge/`, yours to place: the CHANGELOG template's and the release procedure's migration sentence (a release that adds a header key or a record kind says how a running project's records gain it).
+
